4. `rapidcsv::Converter::ToStr`
    `ToStr()` is the value→CSV-string converter in `rapidcsv::Converter`. Use/override it to customize serialization; docs show specializing `Converter<int>` (example overrides `ToVal()`, and README says `ToStr()` can also be overridden). It formats ints/chars directly, float/double with setprecision. Its signature is:
    ```cpp
    /**
     * @brief   Converts numerical value to string representation.
     * @param   pVal                  numerical value
     * @param   pStr                  output string
     */
void ToStr(const T& pVal, std::string& pStr) const;
    ```
5. `rapidcsv::Document::GetRowNames`
    `GetRowNames()` returns all row labels from a CSV when `LabelParams` defines a row-name column. It skips data before the header row. Use it like `GetColumnNames()` to check names. If no row-name index is set, it returns an empty vector. Its signature is:
    ```cpp
    /**
     * @brief   Get row names
     * @returns vector of row names.
     */
std::vector<std::string> GetRowNames() const;
    ```
6. `rapidcsv::Document::GetColumnIdx`
    Returns the zero-based data-column index for a named header. Use it only when column labels are enabled and names are unique. If the name is missing or labels aren’t set, it returns -1. Duplicate names make the result undefined. Its signature is:
    ```cpp
    /**
     * @brief   Get column index by name. The result is undefined if the document contains
     *          multiple columns sharing the same label name. Column indices exceeding
     *          INT_MAX are not supported.
     * @param   pColumnName           column label name.
     * @returns zero-based column index.
     */
int GetColumnIdx(const std::string& pColumnName) const;
    ```
7. `rapidcsv::Document::GetColumnName`
    Returns the header/name for a zero-based data column. Use it only when `mLabelParams.mColumnNameIdx >= 0`; otherwise it throws `std::out_of_range`. It maps the requested column through `GetDataColumnIndex()` and reads that cell from the column-name row. Its signature is:
    ```cpp
    /**
     * @brief   Get column name
     * @param   pColumnIdx            zero-based column index.
     * @returns column name.
     */
std::string GetColumnName(const size_t pColumnIdx) const;
    ```
8. `rapidcsv::Document::Load`
    Load sets the Document’s path/parse params then reads CSV via `ReadCsv()`. Use it with a file path or binary `istream`; defaults are provided. Docs show file, stream/stringstream, and comment-line skipping examples. Its signature is:
    ```cpp
    /**
     * @brief   Read Document data from file.
     * @param   pPath                 specifies the path of an existing CSV-file to populate the Document
     *                                data with.
     * @param   pLabelParams          specifies which row and column should be treated as labels.
     * @param   pSeparatorParams      specifies which field and row separators should be used.
     * @param   pConverterParams      specifies how invalid numbers (including empty strings) should be
     *                                handled.
     * @param   pLineReaderParams     specifies how special line formats should be treated.
     */
void Load(const std::string& pPath,
              const LabelParams& pLabelParams = LabelParams(),
              const SeparatorParams& pSeparatorParams = SeparatorParams(),
              const ConverterParams& pConverterParams = ConverterParams(),
              const LineReaderParams& pLineReaderParams = LineReaderParams());
/**
     * @brief   Read Document data from stream.
     * @param   pStream               specifies a binary input stream to read CSV data from.
     * @param   pLabelParams          specifies which row and column should be treated as labels.
     * @param   pSeparatorParams      specifies which field and row separators should be used.
     * @param   pConverterParams      specifies how invalid numbers (including empty strings) should be
